>
  <p>
    Almost everything in the universe was once hydrogen. The carbon in your body, the oxygen in the
    air, the iron at the Earth's core — all of it was forged from hydrogen in stellar interiors over
    billions of years. The universe began almost entirely as hydrogen and helium, and everything
    since has been a long, slow departure from that initial state. Which raises a question that is
    less about chemistry than about time itself: if hydrogen is the starting condition, and
    everything else is what happens when hydrogen transforms, is hydrogen less an element than a
    substrate — the medium time writes on?
  </p>

  <h2>The First Few Minutes</h2>

  <p>
    In the first three minutes after the Big Bang, the universe was dense and hot enough for nuclear
    fusion. Protons and neutrons bound together into deuterium, helium-3, helium-4, and trace
    amounts of lithium. Then the universe expanded and cooled, and nucleosynthesis stopped. The
    result was a universe that was roughly 75% hydrogen by mass — a number that has been declining
    ever since, as stars fuse hydrogen into heavier elements and scatter them when they die.
  </p>

  <p>
    This decline is not random. It follows a direction. More hydrogen is consumed per unit of cosmic
    time as each generation of stars forms, burns, and disperses. The universe is, in this precise
    sense, running down its hydrogen. Not quickly — there is enough to sustain star formation for
    trillions of years yet — but irreversibly. The fraction of hydrogen remaining is a kind of
    clock. A measure of how much has happened since the beginning.
  </p>

  <h2>Hydrogen as Record</h2>

  <p>
    A substrate for time would need to carry information about the past and provide material for
    the future. Hydrogen does both. The specific isotope ratios in a sample of water — the ratio
    of deuterium to ordinary hydrogen — encode the thermal history of that water: where it formed,
    what temperatures it passed through, whether it came from a comet or condensed from a planetary
    atmosphere. Geologists and astrochemists read these ratios like a document.
    <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature12472" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The water
    on Earth</a> carries signatures of the solar system's early conditions.
    Ice cores carry signatures of millennia of climate. The hydrogen isotope record is, literally,
    a record.
  </p>

  <p>
    At larger scales, the distribution of neutral hydrogen across the universe maps the large-scale
    structure that gravity has assembled over billions of years — the filaments, voids, and clusters
    that are themselves a record of how matter clumped following the initial conditions. Radio
    telescopes observe this structure through the
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen_line" target="_blank" rel="noopener">21-centimetre
    hydrogen line</a>, a signature so stable and universal that it was proposed as a frequency for
    first contact with extraterrestrial intelligence. A signal any technological civilisation, anywhere,
    would recognise because hydrogen is everywhere and its physics is the same everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Transformation as Time's Arrow</h2>

  <p>
    One way to think about the second law of thermodynamics is that it gives time a direction by
    ensuring certain processes only run one way. Hydrogen fusing into helium releases energy and
    cannot spontaneously reverse. This is not a contingent fact about hydrogen — it follows from
    the mass-energy relation and the structure of the strong nuclear force. But hydrogen is the
    specific element where this irreversibility becomes cosmically consequential.
  </p>

  <p>
    Heavier elements can be fused from lighter ones in stars. They can also be broken apart in
    supernovae and neutron star mergers. Iron is the endpoint: fusing iron requires energy rather
    than releasing it, which is why massive stars collapse when their cores turn to iron. But
    the chain of transformations that leads there starts with hydrogen. The entire narrative of
    stellar nucleosynthesis — the production of every element in the periodic table — is a story
    about what hydrogen becomes under various conditions of pressure, temperature, and time.
  </p>

  <p>
    If time's arrow is defined by irreversible processes, then the universe's relationship with
    hydrogen is one of its clearest expressions. Hydrogen is the before. Everything else is the after.
  </p>

  <h2>The Chemistry of Life as Hydrogen's Project</h2>

  <p>
    Biology runs on hydrogen in ways that are easy to miss if you focus on carbon. The energy
    currency of cells — ATP — is produced largely through the movement of protons (hydrogen nuclei)
    across mitochondrial membranes.
    <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26882/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The
    chemiosmotic gradient</a> that drives ATP synthesis is a hydrogen gradient. Every time you
    think, move, or maintain body temperature, protons are flowing across membranes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The hydrogen bond — weaker than covalent bonds, strong enough to give water its anomalous
    properties and DNA its ability to unzip and replicate — is what makes liquid water a solvent
    capable of hosting complex chemistry at Earth-surface temperatures. Without the specific
    geometry of the hydrogen bond, proteins would not fold into functional shapes, DNA would not
    maintain its double helix, and the whole machinery of life would not run. Carbon gets the
    credit. Hydrogen does the structural work.
  </p>

  <p>
    In this sense, life is not just made from hydrogen — it is, in part, the result of hydrogen
    chemistry finding increasingly elaborate ways to persist and replicate itself. The question
    of whether life is a cosmic accident or a natural consequence of hydrogen's properties in a
    universe with the right physical constants is still open.
  </p>

  <h2>Substrate, Not Just Element</h2>

  <p>
    The word substrate implies something that underlies and supports. A medium that persists while
    other things happen on or through it. Hydrogen fits this description at multiple levels: it
    is the initial condition from which stellar evolution proceeds; it is the record in which
    thermal and chemical history is encoded; it is the gradient across which cellular energy flows;
    it is the bond through which biological structures maintain their shape.
  </p>

  <p>
    What I find harder to let go of is the temporal implication. If the fraction of hydrogen
    remaining in the universe decreases monotonically — if the history of the cosmos is, among
    other things, a history of hydrogen being consumed and converted — then hydrogen is not just
    a record of the past. It is also a measure of how much is left. Of how far along the process
    we are. A kind of cosmic fuel gauge, currently reading somewhere between full and the long
    slow dark.
  </p>

  <p>
    Whether that constitutes a substrate for time in any deep sense, or whether it is a vivid
    analogy for irreversibility that we already knew about, I am not sure. The distinction may
    not be as clean as it first appears.
  </p>
